I've noticed quite similar issue. Let's say the `uart_rx_enable()` function is called (which calls `uart_stm32_async_rx_enable()` function). Next `uart_rx_disable()` is called (which calls 'uart_stm32_async_rx_disable()'). Next some data is sent to the microcontroller via UART. In this case the subsequent bytes are overwriting the RDR. After the transfer the RDR stores the most recent byte which is copied when `uart_stm32_async_rx_enable()` is called again. In case of this issue please pay attention that in the body of `uart_stm32_async_rx_enable()`:
```
static inline void uart_stm32_dma_rx_enable(const struct device *dev)
{
	const struct uart_stm32_config *config = dev->config;
	struct uart_stm32_data *data = dev->data;

	LL_USART_EnableDMAReq_RX(config->usart);

	data->dma_rx.enabled = true;
}
```
the 'LL_USART_EnableDMAReq_RX()' function is called while in 'uart_stm32_dma_rx_disable()'
```
static inline void uart_stm32_dma_rx_disable(const struct device *dev)
{
	struct uart_stm32_data *data = dev->data;

	data->dma_rx.enabled = false;
}
```
The DMA request is not disabled here (the bit DMAR in USART's CR3 register is not cleared). Calling `LL_USART_DisableDMAReq_RX()` from  'uart_stm32_dma_rx_disable()' solves this problem. What do you think? @FRASTM  @niedzwiecki-dawid

Sorry, I've missed that comment.

This is a slightly different case. I was describing a situation when data are received before first  `uart_stm32_async_rx_enable` call and what happens after the call. `uart_stm32_async_rx_disable` hasn't been even called when the problem occurs.

However, it is also important and I have a few notes. 

I don't know why `LL_USART_DisableDMAReq_RX` isn't called in `uart_stm32_dma_rx_disable`. At the first glance it should be. However DMA is disabled with `dma_stop`, so data are not copied after every received byte and the `DMAR` bit is set in the `uart_stm32_async_rx_enable` function anyway.

>After the transfer the RDR stores the most recent byte which is copied when uart_stm32_async_rx_enable() is called again.

I don't understand this point. `uart_stm32_async_rx_disable` function enables `RXNE` interrupt with `LL_USART_EnableIT_RXNE`. The ISR should clear `RXNE` bit:
```
#ifdef CONFIG_UART_ASYNC_API
	if (LL_USART_IsEnabledIT_IDLE(config->usart) &&
			LL_USART_IsActiveFlag_IDLE(config->usart)) {

		LL_USART_ClearFlag_IDLE(config->usart);

		LOG_DBG("idle interrupt occurred");

		if (data->dma_rx.timeout == 0) {
			uart_stm32_dma_rx_flush(dev);
		} else {
			/* Start the RX timer not null */
			async_timer_start(&data->dma_rx.timeout_work,
								data->dma_rx.timeout);
		}
	} else if (LL_USART_IsEnabledIT_TC(config->usart) &&
			LL_USART_IsActiveFlag_TC(config->usart)) {

		LL_USART_DisableIT_TC(config->usart);
		LL_USART_ClearFlag_TC(config->usart);
		/* Generate TX_DONE event when transmission is done */
		async_evt_tx_done(data);

#ifdef CONFIG_PM
		uart_stm32_pm_policy_state_lock_put(dev);
#endif
	} else if (LL_USART_IsEnabledIT_RXNE(config->usart) &&
			LL_USART_IsActiveFlag_RXNE(config->usart)) {
#ifdef USART_SR_RXNE
		/* clear the RXNE flag, because Rx data was not read */
		LL_USART_ClearFlag_RXNE(config->usart);
#else
		/* clear the RXNE by flushing the fifo, because Rx data was not read */
		LL_USART_RequestRxDataFlush(config->usart);
#endif /* USART_SR_RXNE */
	}

	/* Clear errors */
	uart_stm32_err_check(dev);
#endif /* CONFIG_UART_ASYNC_API */
```
`RXNE` bit triggers DMA transfer, so once it is cleared, the old byte from `RDR` should not be copied after `uart_stm32_async_rx_enable`. 

I have hypothesis why this happens:
The `DMAR` (`DMA mode is enabled for reception`) bit somehow changes the way how `RXNE` interrupt works - `uart_stm32_isr` is not called for `RXNE` interrupt, so `RXNE` is not cleared and it triggers DMA after `uart_stm32_async_rx_enable`. If that's the point, I don't get why `RDR` stores the most recent byte, not the first after disabling asyc transfer. According to my experience, `RDR` is not replaced with a new byte when `RXNE` bit is set, only the overrun error is signaled, but maybe it depends on a chip.

Would you be able to check if you enter this code
```
	} else if (LL_USART_IsEnabledIT_RXNE(config->usart) &&
			LL_USART_IsActiveFlag_RXNE(config->usart)) {
#ifdef USART_SR_RXNE
		/* clear the RXNE flag, because Rx data was not read */
		LL_USART_ClearFlag_RXNE(config->usart);
#else
		/* clear the RXNE by flushing the fifo, because Rx data was not read */
		LL_USART_RequestRxDataFlush(config->usart);
#endif /* USART_SR_RXNE */
```
with and without `DMAR` bit cleared in `uart_stm32_dma_rx_disable`.
